#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# eigenvalues

#% text
# `eigenvalues(m)` gives the list of the eigenvalues of the square matrix `m`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# Each eigenvalue appears as often as its algebraic multiplicity.

#% item
# Numerical eigenvalues come in order of decreasing absolute value.

#% item
# Exact matrices are solved through the factored characteristic polynomial; roots of irreducible factors of degree 3 or more are given as `root` objects, which `n` evaluates.

#% item
# Machine matrices give machine eigenvalues, complex where needed.

#% item
# A matrix that is not square gives `failure(not_square, eigenvalues)`.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
eigenvalues([[2, 1], [1, 2]])
#% output
#= [3, 1]

#% cell
eigenvalues([[1, 2], [3, 4]])
#% output
#= [5/2 + (1/2)*33^(1/2), 5/2 - (1/2)*33^(1/2)]

#% text
# A rotation has complex eigenvalues:

#% cell
eigenvalues([[0, 1], [-1, 0]])
#% output
#= [im, -im]

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# A repeated eigenvalue:

#% cell
eigenvalues([[1, 1], [0, 1]])
#% output
#= [1, 1]

#% text
# A symbolic matrix:

#% cell
eigenvalues([[a, b], [b, a]])
#% output
#= [a - b, a + b]

#% text
# A cubic characteristic polynomial without rational roots, and its numerical values:

#% cell
eigenvalues([[2, 1, 1], [1, 3, 1], [1, 1, 4]])
#% output
#= [root(&(-17 + 23*&1 - 9*&1^2 + &1^3), 3), root(&(-17 + 23*&1 - 9*&1^2 + &1^3), 2), root(&(-17 + 23*&1 - 9*&1^2 + &1^3), 1)]

#% cell
n(eigenvalues([[2, 1, 1], [1, 3, 1], [1, 1, 4]]))
#% output
#= [5.214319743377535, 2.460811127189111, 1.3248691294333539]

#% text
# A machine matrix:

#% cell
eigenvalues([[1.0, 2.0], [3.0, 4.0]])
#% output
#= [5.372281323269014, -0.3722813232690134]

#% text
# A permutation matrix has the cube roots of unity as eigenvalues:

#% cell
eigenvalues([[0, 1, 0], [0, 0, 1], [1, 0, 0]])
#% output
#= [1, -1/2 + (im/2)*3^(1/2), -1/2 - (im/2)*3^(1/2)]

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# The product of the eigenvalues is the determinant, and their sum is the trace:

#% cell
m = [[4, 1], [2, 3]]; [product(eigenvalues(m)), det(m)]
#% output
#= [10, 10]

#% cell
[sum(eigenvalues(m)), trace(m)]
#% output
#= [7, 7]

#% text
# The eigenvalues are the roots of the characteristic polynomial:

#% cell
solve(det(m - x*identity_matrix(2)) == 0, x)
#% output
#= [[x -> 2], [x -> 5]]

#% subsection
# Possible issues

#% text
# Matrices of balls are not handled; use exact entries and `n` afterwards:

#% cell
eigenvalues(n([[2, 1], [1, 3]], 25))
#% output
#= [3.6180339887498948482045868344, 1.38196601125010515179541316563]

#% cell
n(eigenvalues([[2, 1], [1, 3]]), 25)
#% output
#= [3.6180339887498948482045868344, 1.3819660112501051517954131656]

#% text
# Machine eigenvalues carry rounding errors:

#% cell
eigenvalues([[0.0, 1.0], [-1.0, 0.0]])
#% output
#= [0.0 + 1.0im, 0.0 - 0.9999999999999996im]

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `eigenvectors`, `eigensystem`, `det`, `trace`, `singular_values`
